q","stimulus":"<p class=\"text-center\"><img height=\"305\" src=\"https://www.sciencenewsforstudents.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/1030_SS_amoeba.png\" style=\"width: 542px; height: 305px;\" width=\"542\" /></p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>Amoebas are microscopic organisms that live mostly in water.&nbsp; Amoebas reproduce by binary fission, a fancy word that means splitting in two. When an amoeba begins to divide, it pulls in to form a kind of ball. After its nucleus doubles, the amoeba constricts in the middle, as if a belt were being pulled tighter and tighter around the cell. Finally, the two new cells pinch apart, spread out, and slink away from each other. In this way, two identical “daughter” cells are created from one. When conditions are right, this amoeba can asexually reproduce every 48 hours.</strong></span></p>\n\n<p><span class=\"learnosity-feature\" data-alt=\"An Audio Clip\" data-label=\"Audio\" data-player=\"bar\" data-simplefeature_id=\"cc4e0ac6-4038-5c24-986a-28ee671bb095_1617022d475d5af37cd1245b11494410\" data-src=\"https://assets.learnosity.com/organisations/154/5f3d1cf3-4e5c-440b-82a8-78a5d6cc3ecb.mp3\" data-type=\"audioplayer\"></span></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\">Do amoebas reproduce sexually, asexually or both?</span></p>\n","options":[{"label":"<span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\">Sexually</span>","value":"0"},{"label":"<span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\">Asexually</span>","value":"1"},{"label":"<span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\">Both Sexually and Asexually</span>","value":"2"}],"ui_style":{"choice_label":"upper-alpha","type":"horizontal"},"validation":{"scoring_type":"exactMatch","valid_response":{"score":100,"value":["1"]},"penalty":100},"metadata":{"requires_correction_if_false":false,"requires_correction_if_false_text":"","valid_response_count":1,"sheet_reference":"5a9245b5-a0c2-513d-aa87-d27c0f9cfaca","widget_reference":"5a9245b5-a0c2-513d-aa87-d27c0f9cfaca_question","source":{"organisation_id"